--TEST--
`Core\Http\Socket::close` ends the conversation in both directions and is answered the same way twice — `rule:http-server/an-outbound-socket-belongs-to-the-task-that-opened-it`, `rule:testing/an-outbound-socket-is-answered-by-a-scripted-peer`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Http\Socket::close
// covers: Core\Http\Socket::protocol
// A bound asserted on both sides: the last thing a closed socket still does,
// and the first thing it refuses. A peer with frames left over is what makes
// the reading after the close a decision rather than the end of the script
// arriving anyway.
Core\Test::answerSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/feed", ["first", "never read"]);

var $socket = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/feed");
echo "before: ", $socket->receive()?->text() ?? "-", "\n";
$socket->close();

// Reading is not an error after a close — the conversation is over, and `null`
// is the answer that already means that.
if ($socket->receive() == null) {
    echo "after: null\n";
}

// Writing is, and both send members say the same thing: the refusal is about
// the socket rather than about the payload kind.
try {
    $socket->send("late");
} catch (LogicError $text) {
    echo $text->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $socket->sendBytes("late" as bytes);
} catch (LogicError $octets) {
    echo $octets->message, "\n";
}

// Closing twice is not an error: a program ending a conversation it has
// already ended has made no mistake a refusal could tell it about. The
// subprotocol is still readable, because it is what the handshake settled and
// not a property of the connection being open.
$socket->close();
echo "protocol=", $socket->protocol() ?? "none", "\n";
--EXPECT--
before: first
after: null
Core\Http\Socket::send(): this socket was closed, so there is nobody left to send to
Core\Http\Socket::sendBytes(): this socket was closed, so there is nobody left to send to
protocol=none
